  "promotion": {
    "criterion": "M3 must beat M0 with a paired CI excluding zero",
    "beats_m0_pair_level": true,
    "beats_m0_disease_clustered": true,
    "criterion_met": true,
    "promoted_to_production": false,
    "gene_property_features_add_nothing": true,
    "gain_is_from_effort_features": true,
    "note": "No production score was changed by this run. The criterion (M3 beats M0 with a CI excluding zero) is met, but it is met by features recording prior medicinal chemistry effort, not by gene properties: M4, which keeps only the gene properties, does not improve on M1 (CI includes zero). Promoting a score dominated by existing compound count would rank highest the targets the field has already chosen, which is the Goodhart failure this benchmark exists to catch."
  },
  "finding": "**No gene-property feature tested here predicts clinical progression beyond attention. What does predict it is prior effort — and the strongest signal is the number of compounds that already exist for the target.**\n\nM3, which adds all the pre-2021 tractability features, does beat publication count: held-out ROC-AUC 0.807 against 0.667, a paired delta of +0.140 (95% CI +0.100 to +0.181 resampling pairs; +0.089 to +0.187 resampling diseases). Both intervals exclude zero, so the brief's promotion criterion is formally met.\n\nIt is met by the wrong features. Splitting the tractability set in two — features that record medicinal chemistry or clinical work already done on a target (\"effort\"), and properties of the gene that hold whether or not anyone has studied it (\"biology\") — M4 keeps only the gene properties: pLI, LOEUF, three DepMap essentiality measures and two GTEx selectivity measures. M4 scores 0.719, against M1's 0.721. The paired delta is −0.002 (95% CI −0.026 to +0.022), which includes zero: the five gene-property groups together add nothing over publication count and the eight Open Targets evidence scores. All of M3's advantage comes from the effort features (M3 vs M1 +0.086, 95% CI +0.052 to +0.119), and the single strongest predictor in the whole study is `tract_sm_high_quality_compounds` at solo held-out ROC-AUC 0.772 — higher than any model in Task 3 or Task 4. The best gene property is `pli` at 0.621.\n\nOne correction to the obvious reading, from Table 7. Compound count is NOT a restatement of publication count: regressed against `log1p(publication_count)` it has an R² of 0.002, and residualised against attention it still scores 0.699. So it is a genuinely independent predictor, not attention measured a second way. What it is not is biology: it is a record of medicinal chemistry already done. The answer to \"does biology predict progression beyond attention\" is still no; what beats attention is a different measure of prior human effort.\n\nA target with a hundred high-quality compounds and no drug is one the field has already chosen. Predicting that it enters the clinic is not target discovery.\n\n**No production score is promoted.** A score dominated by existing compound count would rank highest precisely the targets a user could have found without it, and would rank an under-studied target last by construction. That is the Goodhart failure this benchmark exists to catch, and it is why the criterion being met is not sufficient.",
